    id:'npqb-ps-76', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'ps-child',
    stem:'Which is a characteristic feature of intellectual disability?',
    options:['Deficits in social communication','Deficits in general mental abilities such as reasoning and problem-solving','Presence of restricted, repetitive patterns of behavior','Sudden onset of motor tics'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'**Intellectual disability is defined first by deficits in intellectual functions — reasoning, problem-solving and the rest of that list — so option b is the only one that reproduces the disorder\'s own criterion.**\n\n`L14) Child Psychiatry` prints the DSM-5 definition whole. The disorder has *"onset during the developmental period"* and *"includes both intellectual and adaptive functioning deficits in conceptual, social, and practical domains"*, and *"The following three criteria must be met"*. Criterion A is the one keyed here: *"Deficits in intellectual functions, such as reasoning, problem solving, planning, abstract thinking, judgment, academic learning, and learning from experience, confirmed by both clinical assessment and individualized, standardized intelligence testing."* Option b is that first clause in shorter words.\n\nHold the other two criteria beside it, because the bank does not return to intellectual disability anywhere else in this topic. Criterion B is *"Deficits in adaptive functioning that result in failure to meet developmental and sociocultural standards for personal independence and social responsibility"*, limiting *"communication, social participation, and independent living, across multiple environments, such as home, school, work, and community"*. Criterion C is *"Onset of intellectual and adaptive deficits during the developmental period"*. Severity is then graded on IQ — *"Mild (IQ 50 to 70)"*, *"Moderate (IQ 35 to 49)"*, *"Severe (IQ 20 to 34)"*, *"Profound (IQ <20)"* — though this stem prints no number and needs none.\n\n**Note the shape of the distractor menu**, because this topic reuses it. Options a, c and d are each the defining feature of a *different* member of the same DSM-5 neurodevelopmental group, which `L14` lists as *"Intellectual Disorders · Communication Disorders · Autism Spectrum Disorder · Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder · Specific Learning Disorders · Motor Disorders · Tic Disorders · Other Neurodevelopmental Disorders"*. Q78 and Q87 are built from the same pool of strings — answer each on the feature that is *definitional*, not merely possible.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Deficits in social communication** | Belongs to autism spectrum disorder — `L14`\'s criterion 1, *"Persistent deficits in social communication and social interaction across multiple contexts"* — and to social (pragmatic) communication disorder, *"Persistent difficulties in the social use of verbal and nonverbal communication"*. Social difficulty in intellectual disability is a consequence of the intellectual deficit, not the defining criterion |\n| **Presence of restricted, repetitive patterns of behavior** | Autism\'s criterion 2 verbatim, with its four printed sub-items — *"Stereotyped or repetitive motor movements"*, *"Insistence on sameness"*, *"Highly restricted, fixated interests"* and *"Hyper- or hypo reactivity to sensory input"*. Nothing in the intellectual disability criteria mentions repetitive behaviour |\n| **Sudden onset of motor tics** | The tic disorders. `L14` defines a tic as *"a sudden, rapid, recurrent, nonrhythmic motor movement or vocalization"* — a separate category on the same list, and the option that recurs as filler through this whole topic |\n\n**Bottom line:** intellectual disability = intellectual deficit + adaptive deficit + developmental onset, confirmed by clinical assessment *and* standardised testing. Only option b names one of those three.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Intellectual disability requires all three criteria — deficits in intellectual functions such as reasoning and problem-solving, deficits in adaptive functioning across multiple environments, and onset during the developmental period — with severity graded Mild (IQ 50-70) to Profound (IQ <20).',
