    answer:3,
    explanation:'The bank prints an explanation box for this question. **Transcribed verbatim as printed:**\n\n> Tourette\'s disorder is a tic disorder, not a communication disorder. Communication disorders include language disorder, speech sound disorder, and childhood-onset fluency disorder.\n\n**A negative stem: three of these are on one slide and the fourth is on another.** `L14) Child Psychiatry` prints the membership list under the heading *"DSM-5: The Communication Disorders"* — *"Language Disorder"*, *"Speech Sound Disorder"*, *"Social Pragmatic Disorder"* and *"Childhood-onset Fluency Disorder (Stuttering)"*. Options a, b and c are three of those four. Tourette\'s disorder is not there; `L14` files it under *"Tic Disorders"*, a separate member of the DSM-5 neurodevelopmental group.\n\n**⚠️ The printed box lists only three communication disorders and the lecture lists four.** The box omits **social (pragmatic) communication disorder**, which `L14` gives its own slide — *"Persistent difficulties in the social use of verbal and nonverbal communication"*. The omission does not change the key, since the missing member is not among the options, but do not learn the box as a complete list.\n\n**What makes the category coherent** is the distinction `L14` draws before it lists the members: *"Speech is the expressive production of sounds and includes an individual\'s articulation, fluency, voice, and resonance quality"*, while *"Language includes the form, function, and use of a conventional system of symbols (i.e., spoken words, sign language, written words, pictures) in a rule-governed manner for communication."* Every communication disorder is a failure of one of those two systems. A tic is a failure of neither — it is a movement.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong as the exception** — that is, why each genuinely is a communication disorder:\n\n| Option | Why it is a communication disorder |\n|---|---|\n| **Language disorder** | First on the printed DSM-5 list. It is a disorder of *language* in `L14`\'s sense — the symbol system itself, its form, function and rule-governed use |\n| **Speech sound disorder** | Second on the list, and a disorder of *speech* — the articulation half of the deck\'s definition, the production of sounds |\n| **Childhood-onset fluency disorder** | Fourth on the list, printed with its familiar name in brackets, *"(Stuttering)"*, and given its own slide: *"Disturbances in the normal fluency and time patterning of speech that are inappropriate for the individual\'s age and language skills, persist over time."* Note it disturbs fluency, which the deck counts as part of speech |\n| **Tourette\'s disorder** *(the keyed answer)* | Not on the list at all. `L14` defines a tic as *"a sudden, rapid, recurrent, nonrhythmic motor movement or vocalization"* and requires for Tourette\'s that *"Both multiple motor and one or more vocal tics have been present"*, persisting *"for more than 1 year"*, with *"Onset … before age 18 years"*. The vocal tics are the trap — a vocal tic is a noise, not disordered language |\n\n**Bottom line:** vocal tics involve the voice but not the language system; Tourette\'s is a tic disorder, and the communication disorders are language, speech sound, social pragmatic and childhood-onset fluency disorder.',
